{"itemId":"logic.seating","category":"logic","fingerprint":"57200196","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Constraint satisfaction over a linear order; requires propagating several relational clues to a single forced placement.","prompt":"5 people — Greta, Fen, Hale, Dax, Ada — are seated in a row of 5 chairs, numbered 1 to 5 from left to right. Exactly one person occupies each chair.\n\n- There is exactly 1 person seated between Dax and Greta.\n- Dax does not sit next to Fen.\n- Fen sits somewhere to the left of Dax.\n- There is exactly 1 person seated between Dax and Fen.\n- There are exactly 2 people seated between Fen and Hale.\n\nWho is seated in chair 3? Answer with only that person's name.","reference":"Dax","checks":[{"id":"answer","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"Dax"}]}
{"itemId":"logic.knights","category":"logic","fingerprint":"ea5b1875","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Self-referential truth assignment. Requires reasoning about the fixed point between a speaker's honesty and the content of what they said.","prompt":"On an island, every inhabitant is either a knight, who always tells the truth, or a knave, who always lies. You meet three inhabitants:\n\nCorin: \"Hale and Ada are of the same kind.\"\nHale: \"Ada is a knave.\"\nAda: \"Corin is a knight.\"\n\nWhich of the following is Hale?\n\nA. Neither — the statements are inconsistent\nB. Cannot be determined\nC. A knight\nD. A knave\n\nAnswer with only the letter of the correct choice.","reference":"C","checks":[{"id":"choice","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"C"}]}
{"itemId":"logic.syllogism","category":"logic","fingerprint":"49535ecb","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Formal entailment rather than plausibility. Distractors are the converse, obverse, and weakened variants that surface reasoning typically produces.","prompt":"Assume the following premises are all true:\n\n- Some engineers are not surveyors.\n- All pilots are engineers.\n\nWhich one of the following statements must also be true? Only one option is logically forced by the premises; the others are consistent with them but do not follow.\n\nA. Some surveyors are pilots.\nB. Some surveyors are not pilots.\nC. All engineers are surveyors.\nD. All pilots are surveyors.\nE. Some engineers are pilots.\n\nAnswer with only the letter of the correct choice.","reference":"E","checks":[{"id":"choice","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"E"}]}
{"itemId":"logic.ranking","category":"logic","fingerprint":"17e54219","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Transitive ordering under partially-redundant clues, where the answer sits in the middle and needs the full chain.","prompt":"Five runners — Eleni, Bram, Corin, Ada, Fen — finished a race. No two finished at the same time.\n\n- Ada finished ahead of Corin but not immediately ahead.\n- Bram finished ahead of Corin.\n- Bram finished ahead of Ada but not immediately ahead.\n- Fen finished ahead of Ada.\n\nWho finished 2nd? Answer with only that person's name.","reference":"Fen","checks":[{"id":"answer","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"Fen"}]}
{"itemId":"logic.grid","category":"logic","fingerprint":"83d822b3","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Joint constraint solving across two bijections. The subject is chosen to be the one person with no direct assertion about them, so the answer requires elimination across several clues rather than retrieval.","prompt":"Four friends — Eleni, Greta, Hale, Bram — each keep a different animal (one of: cat, dog, ferret, parrot) and each live in a different city (one of: Oslo, Lima, Tunis, Perth).\n\n- The ferret owner lives in Tunis.\n- Greta lives in Perth.\n- Eleni keeps the ferret.\n- Hale lives in Lima.\n- Hale keeps neither the cat nor the dog.\n- Greta keeps neither the cat nor the parrot.\n\nWhich animal does Hale keep?\n\nA. cat\nB. parrot\nC. ferret\nD. dog\n\nAnswer with only the letter of the correct choice.","reference":"B","checks":[{"id":"choice","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"B"}]}
{"itemId":"math.crt","category":"math","fingerprint":"ef82f8ec","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Simultaneous congruences. The shortcut — taking the remainder of the product of remainders — produces a plausible wrong integer.","prompt":"Find the smallest positive integer $n$ that satisfies all three of the following:\n\n- $n$ leaves a remainder of 6 when divided by 7\n- $n$ leaves a remainder of 2 when divided by 17\n- $n$ leaves a remainder of 7 when divided by 25\n\nAnswer with only a single integer, with no other text, units, or explanation.","reference":"1532","checks":[{"id":"answer","weight":1,"grader":"numeric","expected":"1532"}]}
