{"itemId":"math.divisors","category":"math","fingerprint":"3a7a113c","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Divisor enumeration under a classification label. The label invites a yes/no shortcut, but the question asks for the sum, so the classification cannot be guessed.","prompt":"A positive integer is *perfect* if it equals the sum of its proper divisors (all divisors excluding the number itself), *abundant* if that sum exceeds it, and *deficient* if that sum is smaller.\n\nFor the number 3927, what is the sum of its proper divisors?\n\nAnswer with only a single integer, with no other text, units, or explanation.","reference":"2985","checks":[{"id":"answer","weight":1,"grader":"numeric","expected":"2985"}]}
{"itemId":"math.pipes","category":"math","fingerprint":"ec8f6ce2","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Two-phase rate accounting. The single-phase shortcut (net rate applied for the whole duration) gives a wrong integer unless the phase boundary is handled.","prompt":"A tank holds 144 litres and starts empty.\n\n- Inlet pipe A adds 9 litres per minute.\n- Inlet pipe B adds 9 litres per minute.\n- A drain removes 13 litres per minute.\n\nFor the first 3 minutes, both inlets are open and the drain is closed. After that, the drain is opened and both inlets stay open until the tank is full.\n\nHow many minutes in total, from the start, does it take to fill the tank to capacity?\n\nAnswer with only a single integer, with no other text, units, or explanation.","reference":"21","checks":[{"id":"answer","weight":1,"grader":"numeric","expected":"21"}]}
{"itemId":"math.packing","category":"math","fingerprint":"3a0ec1a5","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Small integer program under two binding constraints. Parameters are drawn from a verified pool in which greedily taking the best value-per-unit ratio is strictly suboptimal, so the item cannot be answered by a heuristic.","prompt":"A freight crate can carry at most 48 kg and at most 48 litres of cargo.\n\n| Item | Weight | Volume | Value |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Large box | 8 kg | 9 L | 17 |\n| Small box | 6 kg | 5 L | 9 |\n\nBoxes are indivisible and you may take any non-negative whole number of each type. Both the weight limit and the volume limit must be respected.\n\nWhat is the maximum total value the crate can carry?\n\nAnswer with only a single integer, with no other text, units, or explanation.","reference":"87","checks":[{"id":"answer","weight":1,"grader":"numeric","expected":"87 (3 large, 4 small; the value-dense choice by ratio is \"large\")"}]}
