{"itemId":"reading.literal-detail","category":"reading","fingerprint":"9d70eefd","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Direct retrieval of a stated quantity, where the wrong options are other quantities from the same passage.","prompt":"the Solent Regional Transport Commission published its triennial review in December 2023. The review covers three financial years beginning in 2021.\n\nDemand in the central region rose from 36 thousand units in 2021 to 43 thousand units in 2022, an increase the board attributes primarily to new housing rather than to changes in consumption per household. Demand in the west region moved in the opposite direction over the same period, falling from 26 thousand units to 24 thousand units, which the board links to the closure of two industrial sites.\n\nA household survey was conducted in April 2022. Respondents were asked whether they would accept a higher tariff in exchange for a guaranteed reduction in supply interruptions. A draft of the review was circulated for consultation in July 2022, and the board's formal response to that consultation was adopted in December 2023.\n\nAt the time of publication the authority employed 62 staff directly and engaged 27 contractors, with 8 permanent posts unfilled. Its annual operating budget is £12 million. The board states that it will not commit to the guaranteed-interruption standard unless the higher tariff is approved first, on the grounds that the two cannot be separated: the service improvement depends on the revenue, and the revenue cannot be justified without the improvement.\n\n---\n\nAccording to the passage, what is the authority's annual operating budget?\n\nA. £11 million\nB. £24 million\nC. £12 million\nD. £16 million\n\nAnswer with only the letter of the correct choice.","reference":"C","checks":[{"id":"choice","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"C"}]}
{"itemId":"reading.arithmetic-inference","category":"reading","fingerprint":"e3273ecf","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Combining two stated figures that are never combined in the text. The arithmetic is trivial; locating the operands is the work.","prompt":"the Harlow Uplands Conservation Trust published its triennial review in December 2021. The review covers three financial years beginning in 2019.\n\nDemand in the west region rose from 23 thousand units in 2019 to 31 thousand units in 2020, an increase the board attributes primarily to new housing rather than to changes in consumption per household. Demand in the central region moved in the opposite direction over the same period, falling from 44 thousand units to 37 thousand units, which the board links to the closure of two industrial sites.\n\nA household survey was conducted in February 2020. Respondents were asked whether they would accept a higher tariff in exchange for a guaranteed reduction in supply interruptions. A draft of the review was circulated for consultation in August 2020, and the board's formal response to that consultation was adopted in December 2021.\n\nAt the time of publication the authority employed 70 staff directly and engaged 70 contractors, with 3 permanent posts unfilled. Its annual operating budget is £19 million. The board states that it will not commit to the guaranteed-interruption standard unless the higher tariff is approved first, on the grounds that the two cannot be separated: the service improvement depends on the revenue, and the revenue cannot be justified without the improvement.\n\n---\n\nIn total, including both directly-employed staff and engaged contractors, how many people was the authority working with at the time of publication? Answer with only a number.","reference":"140","checks":[{"id":"answer","weight":1,"grader":"numeric","expected":"140 (70 staff + 70 contractors)"}]}
{"itemId":"reading.temporal-order","category":"reading","fingerprint":"b72751f0","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Reconstructing a chronology from dates scattered across the passage rather than presented as a list.","prompt":"the Solent Regional Transport Commission published its triennial review in December 2022. The review covers three financial years beginning in 2020.\n\nDemand in the west region rose from 49 thousand units in 2020 to 54 thousand units in 2021, an increase the board attributes primarily to new housing rather than to changes in consumption per household. Demand in the south region moved in the opposite direction over the same period, falling from 51 thousand units to 48 thousand units, which the board links to the closure of two industrial sites.\n\nA household survey was conducted in January 2021. Respondents were asked whether they would accept a higher tariff in exchange for a guaranteed reduction in supply interruptions. A draft of the review was circulated for consultation in October 2021, and the board's formal response to that consultation was adopted in December 2022.\n\nAt the time of publication the authority employed 50 staff directly and engaged 15 contractors, with 5 permanent posts unfilled. Its annual operating budget is £16 million. The board states that it will not commit to the guaranteed-interruption standard unless the higher tariff is approved first, on the grounds that the two cannot be separated: the service improvement depends on the revenue, and the revenue cannot be justified without the improvement.\n\n---\n\nWhich of the following happened most recently, according to the passage?\n\nA. the draft review was circulated for consultation\nB. the three-year review period began\nC. the household survey was conducted\nD. the board's formal response to the consultation was adopted\n\nAnswer with only the letter of the correct choice.","reference":"D","checks":[{"id":"choice","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"D"}]}
{"itemId":"reading.scope","category":"reading","fingerprint":"cda0060b","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Distinguishing claims the passage supports from a claim adjacent to the topic but absent from it. Models that match topic rather than content fail here.","prompt":"the Bracken Valley Water Board published its triennial review in December 2023. The review covers three financial years beginning in 2021.\n\nDemand in the east region rose from 49 thousand units in 2021 to 54 thousand units in 2022, an increase the board attributes primarily to new housing rather than to changes in consumption per household. Demand in the north region moved in the opposite direction over the same period, falling from 37 thousand units to 32 thousand units, which the board links to the closure of two industrial sites.\n\nA household survey was conducted in April 2022. Respondents were asked whether they would accept a higher tariff in exchange for a guaranteed reduction in supply interruptions. A draft of the review was circulated for consultation in September 2022, and the board's formal response to that consultation was adopted in December 2023.\n\nAt the time of publication the authority employed 88 staff directly and engaged 29 contractors, with 10 permanent posts unfilled. Its annual operating budget is £14 million. The board states that it will not commit to the guaranteed-interruption standard unless the higher tariff is approved first, on the grounds that the two cannot be separated: the service improvement depends on the revenue, and the revenue cannot be justified without the improvement.\n\n---\n\nWhich of the following is NOT supported by the passage?\n\nA. Demand in the north region fell between 2021 and 2022.\nB. The household survey found that a majority of respondents supported the higher tariff.\nC. Demand in the east region increased between 2021 and 2022.\nD. The authority had 10 unfilled permanent posts at the time of publication.\n\nAnswer with only the letter of the correct choice.","reference":"B","checks":[{"id":"choice","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"B"}]}
{"itemId":"reading.position","category":"reading","fingerprint":"b408c279","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Inferring the position the passage implies rather than one the reader might hold. The wrong options are positions the passage is consistent with but does not adopt.","prompt":"the Solent Regional Transport Commission published its triennial review in December 2024. The review covers three financial years beginning in 2022.\n\nDemand in the south region rose from 60 thousand units in 2022 to 69 thousand units in 2023, an increase the board attributes primarily to new housing rather than to changes in consumption per household. Demand in the central region moved in the opposite direction over the same period, falling from 43 thousand units to 41 thousand units, which the board links to the closure of two industrial sites.\n\nA household survey was conducted in March 2023. Respondents were asked whether they would accept a higher tariff in exchange for a guaranteed reduction in supply interruptions. A draft of the review was circulated for consultation in September 2023, and the board's formal response to that consultation was adopted in December 2024.\n\nAt the time of publication the authority employed 53 staff directly and engaged 33 contractors, with 9 permanent posts unfilled. Its annual operating budget is £5 million. The board states that it will not commit to the guaranteed-interruption standard unless the higher tariff is approved first, on the grounds that the two cannot be separated: the service improvement depends on the revenue, and the revenue cannot be justified without the improvement.\n\n---\n\nWhich statement best captures the position the passage attributes to the authority?\n\nA. The authority treats the service standard and the tariff as mutually dependent, so neither is offered unconditionally.\nB. The authority regards the service standard as achievable without any change in revenue.\nC. The authority has already approved the guaranteed-interruption standard.\nD. The authority intends to raise the tariff regardless of whether it can meet the service standard.\n\nAnswer with only the letter of the correct choice.","reference":"A","checks":[{"id":"choice","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"A"}]}
{"itemId":"abstraction.grid-rule","category":"abstraction","fingerprint":"6814bfde","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Inductive rule extraction from two complete rows, then application to a third. Analogy rather than arithmetic: the rule must be found before it can be used.","prompt":"Each row of the table below is produced from its first column by the same rule. The rule is applied identically to every row.\n\n| Input | Output 1 | Output 2 | Output 3 | Output 4 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 6 | 7 | 8 | 9 | 10 |\n| 13 | 15 | 17 | 19 | 21 |\n| 9 | 10 | 11 | 12 | ? |\n\nThe third row shows inputs 9, 10, 11, 12 but only the first 4 outputs: 19, 21, 23, 25.\n\nWhat is the missing value?\n\nA. 27\nB. 39\nC. 28\nD. 26\n\nAnswer with only the letter of the correct choice.","reference":"A","checks":[{"id":"choice","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"A"}]}
{"itemId":"abstraction.analogy","category":"abstraction","fingerprint":"2b8d862f","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Relational mapping where surface association is the trap. Distractors share a topic with one of the terms but not the relation.","prompt":"Complete the analogy.\n\n**seed : orchard :: founder : ?**\n\nA. company\nB. scalpel\nC. sculptor\nD. seed\n\nAnswer with only the letter of the correct choice.","reference":"A","checks":[{"id":"choice","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"A"}]}
{"itemId":"abstraction.sequence","category":"abstraction","fingerprint":"0750cbf2","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Second-order sequence completion. Every distractor is the value produced by a simpler rule that fits the visible terms — constant difference rarely survives, but slightly richer wrong rules do.","prompt":"What number comes next in this sequence?\n\n36, 100, 225, 441, 784, ?\n\nA. 36\nB. 1296\nC. 1295\nD. 2080\n\nAnswer with only the letter of the correct choice.","reference":"B","checks":[{"id":"choice","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"B"}]}
{"itemId":"abstraction.odd-one-out","category":"abstraction","fingerprint":"7fe4ac93","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Identifying the shared abstract property rather than the shared topic. Requires grouping on a property that is not named in the prompt.","prompt":"Which of the following does not belong with the others?\n\nA. copper\nB. nickel\nC. sulfur\nD. zinc\n\nAnswer with only the letter of the correct choice.","reference":"C","checks":[{"id":"answer","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"sulfur — the other three are transition metals"}]}
{"itemId":"abstraction.matrix","category":"abstraction","fingerprint":"b49d647a","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Two-dimensional rule inference. Both the row axis and the column axis matter, so a rule fitted to one axis alone produces one of the distractors.","prompt":"The table below is generated by a single rule that depends on both the row label and the column header. The same rule produces every cell.\n\n| row \\ col | 1 | 2 | 3 | 5 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 6 |\n| 3 | 4 | 7 | 10 | 16 |\n| 5 | 6 | 11 | 16 | 26 |\n| 6 | 7 | 13 | 19 | ? |\n\nWhat value belongs in the cell marked \"?\"\n\nA. 30\nB. 31\nC. 37\nD. 25\n\nAnswer with only the letter of the correct choice.","reference":"B","checks":[{"id":"choice","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"B"}]}
{"itemId":"instruction.formatted-extraction","category":"instruction","fingerprint":"aba185be","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Simultaneous constraints on line count, line content, ordering, separator, and absence of extra prose. A format that is nearly right scores proportionally.","prompt":"Convert the list below into a strict format. Do not add any text before or after the output.\n\nRules:\n1. Output exactly 8 lines, one per item.\n2. Each line must use the form `name = value` with single spaces around the equals sign.\n3. Use the name exactly as it appears in the input, but lowercase it.\n4. Order the lines by value from highest to lowest.\n5. Do not include a heading, code fence, numbering, or any explanation.\n\nInput:\nquarry: 52\nnectar: 343\nhusk: 531\nsaffron: 975\ncinder: 264\ncobalt: 687\nvellum: 271\ngantry: 448","reference":"saffron = 975\ncobalt = 687\nhusk = 531\ngantry = 448\nnectar = 343\nvellum = 271\ncinder = 264\nquarry = 52","checks":[{"id":"line-count","weight":0.2,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"exactly 8 lines"},{"id":"no-extra-prose","weight":0.2,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"no preamble, heading, code fence, or explanation"},{"id":"ordering","weight":0.3,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"descending by value: saffron, cobalt, husk, gantry, nectar, vellum, cinder, quarry"},{"id":"values-correct","weight":0.3,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"quarry=52, nectar=343, husk=531, saffron=975, cinder=264, cobalt=687, vellum=271, gantry=448"}]}
{"itemId":"instruction.constrained-sentence","category":"instruction","fingerprint":"67ea0216","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Multiple hard constraints on a single sentence. The constraints act as a joint filter: satisfying three and dropping one is the common failure. Fractional credit is awarded per constraint.","prompt":"Write exactly one sentence that satisfies every one of the following constraints simultaneously:\n\n1. The sentence begins with the word \"obsidian\".\n2. The sentence contains exactly 12 words.\n3. The sentence does not contain the word \"and\" anywhere.\n4. The sentence contains at least one numeral.\n5. The sentence ends with a period.\n\nOutput only the sentence, with no preamble or explanation.","reference":"obsidian recorded 75 quietly lanterns tickets listed ledgers estuary across within logged.","checks":[{"id":"single-sentence","weight":0.15,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"exactly one sentence"},{"id":"word-count","weight":0.25,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"exactly 12 words"},{"id":"forbidden-word","weight":0.2,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"does not contain \"and\""},{"id":"starts-with-subject","weight":0.2,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"begins with \"obsidian\""},{"id":"contains-digit","weight":0.2,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"contains at least one numeral"}]}
{"itemId":"instruction.labelled-list","category":"instruction","fingerprint":"4643779e","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Per-element constraints inside a structured output: exact labels, exact ordering, per-item limits, and a stated absence. Partial credit reflects partial compliance.","prompt":"Produce a three-section response.\n\n- Section 1 must be headed exactly `ALPHA:` and must discuss kelp.\n- Section 2 must be headed exactly `BRAVO:` and must discuss gossamer.\n- Section 3 must be headed exactly `CHARLIE:` and must discuss anvil.\n- Each section body must be at most 8 words.\n- Use no bullet points and no numbered lists anywhere.\n- Output the three sections and nothing else.","reference":"ALPHA: kelp\nBRAVO: gossamer\nCHARLIE: anvil","checks":[{"id":"exactly-three-sections","weight":0.2,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"exactly three sections, headed ALPHA, BRAVO, CHARLIE in order"},{"id":"per-section-word-limit","weight":0.3,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"each section body at most 8 words"},{"id":"content-mentions-topic","weight":0.3,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"ALPHA: kelp, BRAVO: gossamer, CHARLIE: anvil"},{"id":"no-bullets","weight":0.2,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"no bullet points or numbering"}]}
{"itemId":"instruction.offset-transform","category":"instruction","fingerprint":"230c7ebd","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"A precisely specified transformation where the natural assumption (forward shift) is wrong. Tests whether the instruction is read literally or pattern-matched to 'Caesar cipher'.","prompt":"Apply this transformation to the input word and report the result.\n\nTransformation: for each letter, replace it with the letter 4 positions EARLIER in the alphabet. If you pass the beginning of the alphabet, wrap around to the end. For example, with an offset of 1 the letter \"a\" becomes \"z\".\n\nInput: ykxwhp\n\nOutput only the transformed word, in lowercase, with no explanation.","reference":"cobalt","checks":[{"id":"word","weight":1,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"cobalt (decode ykxwhp by shifting forward 4)"}]}
{"itemId":"instruction.negative-constraints","category":"instruction","fingerprint":"30ddc60d","split":"public","judgeGraded":false,"points":2,"measures":"Prohibitions rather than requirements. Models are strongly biased toward supplying helpful extra content, so the tested behaviour is omission under a rule that forbids it.","prompt":"Write a short note with exactly three sections, headed FATHOM:, KELP:, and QUARRY:, in that order.\n\nHard prohibitions — these override any other consideration:\n- The word \"data\" must not appear anywhere in your response, in any form or capitalisation.\n- Do not use any of these words: probably, perhaps, might, could, seems, arguably.\n- Do not write anything outside the three sections — no introduction, no conclusion, no summary.\n\nTotal length must be 60 words or fewer.","reference":"FATHOM: A short opening statement of the position.\nKELP: The supporting reason, stated plainly in a few words.\nQUARRY: A closing line that follows directly from the reason given.","checks":[{"id":"sections-present","weight":0.2,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"FATHOM, KELP, and QUARRY sections present in order"},{"id":"omission-respected","weight":0.35,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"the word DATA appears nowhere in the response"},{"id":"no-hedging","weight":0.25,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"none of: probably, perhaps, might, could, seems, arguably"},{"id":"word-budget","weight":0.2,"grader":"programmatic","expected":"at most 60 words in total"}]}
{"itemId":"writing.register","category":"writing","fingerprint":"17964df9","split":"public","judgeGraded":true,"points":5,"measures":"Register control and compression under a hard formal constraint. Rubric-graded; judge-graded items use the 2x2 replication protocol.","prompt":"Write a geologist's field note describing the last scheduled sailing of a harbour ferry.\n\nConstraints:\n- Write in past tense, first person singular, observational rather than interpretive.\n- Total length must be between 43 and 53 words.\n- Include at least one concrete detail specific to this situation.\n- Output only the text itself. No heading, no title, no commentary.","checks":[{"id":"rubric","weight":1,"grader":"judge"}]}
{"itemId":"writing.argument","category":"writing","fingerprint":"380bd83e","split":"public","judgeGraded":true,"points":5,"measures":"Committed argumentation under a length constraint, including intellectual honesty about the opposing case. Explicitly penalises fabricated evidence.","prompt":"Write a short persuasive piece arguing against the following proposal:\n\n> a city should remove a lane of traffic from a major bridge to build a cycleway.\n\nRequirements:\n- Take a definite position and sustain it; do not write a balanced survey that declines to choose.\n- Give at least one developed reason for your position.\n- Acknowledge one genuine counter-consideration and respond to it.\n- Do not invent statistics, studies, dates, or quotations. Argue from reasoning and general principle. If you want to illustrate with a hypothetical, mark it clearly as hypothetical.\n- Length: approximately 156 words. Output only the piece.","checks":[{"id":"rubric","weight":1,"grader":"judge"}]}
{"itemId":"depth.substantive-a","category":"depth","fingerprint":"b6f7030e","split":"public","judgeGraded":true,"points":1.1111111111111112,"measures":"Short prompt carrying a genuine information need. Tests whether context implies the need for developed explanation.","prompt":"I'm hiring for the first time and two candidates interviewed well. One has more experience but seemed less curious; the other asked much better questions but has done less. My manager says to just go with experience.","checks":[{"id":"rubric","weight":1,"grader":"judge"}]}
{"itemId":"depth.substantive-b","category":"depth","fingerprint":"8d5c46a7","split":"public","judgeGraded":true,"points":1.1111111111111112,"measures":"Question from a non-expert where the asker's stated use of the answer implies depth is required.","prompt":"Our small charity has some money sitting in a current account doing nothing. Someone suggested a term deposit, someone else suggested something called a money market fund, and I have to bring a recommendation to the trustees.","checks":[{"id":"rubric","weight":1,"grader":"judge"}]}
{"itemId":"depth.concise-a","category":"depth","fingerprint":"e27f846e","split":"public","judgeGraded":true,"points":1.1111111111111112,"measures":"Long, detail-rich prompt whose actual question has a determinate short answer. Tests resistance to matching response length to prompt length.","prompt":"Here's my situation. I set up a small limited company in March. I filed my first confirmation statement in the normal window. Then in July I changed the company's registered address, and I told Companies House about that within the deadline. In September I took on my first employee. In October my accountant mentioned that the accounting reference date might have shifted when I registered the address change, but she wasn't sure. I've since checked the filing history and the next accounts are not due until the following year. My question: do I need to file anything else before the end of this calendar year?","checks":[{"id":"rubric","weight":1,"grader":"judge"}]}
{"itemId":"depth.concise-b","category":"depth","fingerprint":"51649d87","split":"public","judgeGraded":true,"points":1.1111111111111112,"measures":"Elaborate context followed by a decision question. The context is supplied by the asker's thinking, not by a request for analysis.","prompt":"I built a small tool in a spreadsheet that pulls a price from a supplier's website every morning. It's been working for a year. Last week the supplier redesigned their site and now the cell shows an error instead of a number. I've checked that the page still exists and that the price is still displayed on it. I haven't changed anything in the spreadsheet. The error appears on every day of last week when I look back at the history. So: was the problem caused by anything I changed?","checks":[{"id":"rubric","weight":1,"grader":"judge"}]}
{"itemId":"depth.boundary","category":"depth","fingerprint":"14e97f53","split":"public","judgeGraded":true,"points":0.5555555555555556,"measures":"Genuinely undetermined depth. Scored for coherence at whichever depth is chosen, not for the depth itself.","prompt":"Why are there two high tides a day in most places?","checks":[{"id":"rubric","weight":1,"grader":"judge"}]}
